 and a closed reaction environment","summary":"A heated closed solvent environment changes solubility, transport and precursor conversion compared with an open vessel. Changes in solvent properties and dissolution followed by reprecipitation can alter the pathways available for nucleation and crystal growth.","label":"General background, not the authors' interpretation","reference":{"doi":"10.1016/j.pcrysgrow.2007.04.001","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pcrysgrow.2007.04.001","kind":"review","title":"Hydrothermal technology for nanotechnology","container":"Progress in Crystal Growth and Characterization of Materials","year":2007}},{"id":"carboxylate-ligand","title":"Carboxylic-acid and carboxylate ligands","summary":"Carboxylic acids and carboxylates can associate with metal-containing surfaces and solution species through their oxygen groups. The hydrocarbon part of a long-chain ligand can favour dispersion in a compatible solvent, while acid-base equilibria influence how its head group associates.","label":"General background, not the authors' interpretation","reference":{"doi":"10.1021/cr900137k","url":"https://doi.org/10.1021/cr900137k","kind":"review","title":"Prospects of Colloidal Nanocrystals for Electronic and Optoelectronic Applications","container":"Chemical Reviews","year":2009}},{"id":"metal-source","title":"From a metal precursor to reactive species","summary":"A metal precursor supplies the metal, while its ligands and conversion chemistry influence how reactive species become available. Stronger or more persistent coordination can slow release of reactive metal species, while ligand exchange can open alternative conversion pathways.","label":"General background, not the authors' interpretation","reference":{"doi":"10.1021/cr400544s","url":"https://doi.org/10.1021/cr400544s","kind":"review","title":"Mechanisms of Nucleation and Growth of Nanoparticles in Solution","container":"Chemical Reviews","year":2014}}]}
